Specification Payees

Description Turning what the bank calls a payee into what the user calls it, and
Description remembering which category that payee usually takes.
Details
Details A bank writes a payee as a terminal record: HARRIS TEETER #231 DURHAM NC
Details 01/15. A report grouped by that is useless, because every shop appears
Details once per branch and once per visit. The rules here collapse that back to
Details one name, and carry the category that was chosen last time.
Details
Details Nothing here guesses. A rule exists because the user made it, or because
Details the user categorised a transaction and agreed to remember it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The rule
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Entity PayeeRule
Description One rule: what to look for, and what to do when it is found.
| Attribute  | Type        | Default | Notes                                 |
| Pattern    | Text        |         | What is looked for in the raw name    |
| MatchType  | MatchType   | Contains |                                      |
| Payee      | PayeeName   |         | The name to record instead            |
| Category   | AccountPath | none    | Empty leaves the category alone       |
| Enabled    | Boolean     | true    |                                       |

DataType MatchType
Description How a pattern is compared with the raw name from the bank.
Details The comparison ignores case in every form. Contains is the default
Details because it is what a user means by typing part of a name.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value      | Notes                                                |
| Contains   | The pattern appears somewhere in the raw name        |
| StartsWith | The raw name begins with the pattern                 |
| Exact      | The whole raw name equals the pattern                |
| Regex      | A regular expression, for the one case in a hundred  |

Collection PayeeRules
| DataType  | Minimum | Maximum | Notes                     |
| PayeeRule | 0       | 10000   | Pattern need not be unique |

BusinessRule How a pattern is matched
Description Each match type against the same raw name, so the difference is
Description visible in one place.
Examples: MatchCase
| MatchType  | Pattern          | RawName                       | Matches |
| Contains   | HARRIS TEETER    | HARRIS TEETER #231 DURHAM NC  | true    |
| Contains   | harris teeter    | HARRIS TEETER #231 DURHAM NC  | true    |
| Contains   | TEETER           | HARRIS TEETER #231 DURHAM NC  | true    |
| Contains   | WHOLE FOODS      | HARRIS TEETER #231 DURHAM NC  | false   |
| StartsWith | HARRIS           | HARRIS TEETER #231 DURHAM NC  | true    |
| StartsWith | TEETER           | HARRIS TEETER #231 DURHAM NC  | false   |
| Exact      | HARRIS TEETER    | HARRIS TEETER #231 DURHAM NC  | false   |
| Exact      | HARRIS TEETER    | HARRIS TEETER                 | true    |
| Regex      | ^SHELL ?OIL      | SHELLOIL 4471                 | true    |
| Regex      | ^SHELL ?OIL      | ROYAL SHELL OIL               | false   |

Attributes MatchCase
| Attribute | Type      | Default  |
| MatchType | MatchType | Contains |
| Pattern   | String    |          |
| RawName   | String    |          |
| Matches   | Boolean   | false    |

BusinessRule Which rule wins when several match
Description The most specific rule, which is taken to be the one with the longest
Description pattern. Length is a crude measure, and it is used because it needs no
Description explaining: a user who adds a longer pattern expects it to win.
Details Ties are broken by match type, in the order Exact, StartsWith, Regex,
Details Contains, and then by the order the rules were added.
Examples: RulePrecedence
| PatternA      | TypeA    | PatternB         | TypeB    | Winner           | Notes                       |
| AMAZON        | Contains | AMAZON MKTPLACE  | Contains | AMAZON MKTPLACE  | Longer pattern              |
| SHELL         | Contains | SHELL            | Exact    | SHELL            | Same length; Exact wins     |
| HARRIS TEETER | Contains | TEETER           | Contains | HARRIS TEETER    | Longer pattern              |

Attributes RulePrecedence
| Attribute | Type      | Default  |
| PatternA  | String    |          |
| TypeA     | MatchType | Contains |
| PatternB  | String    |          |
| TypeB     | MatchType | Contains |
| Winner    | String    |          |
| Notes     | String    | none     |

Scenario A rule tidies the payee and sets the category
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given payee rules are : PayeeRules
| Pattern       | MatchType | Payee         | Category           |
| HARRIS TEETER | Contains  | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount | FitId       | Name                         | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2024-01-15 | -45.00 | 20240115001 | HARRIS TEETER #231 DURHAM NC | none | none     |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee         | Account            | Amount | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Assets:Checking    | -45.00 | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | 45.00  | Uncleared |

Scenario A rule with no category tidies the name only
Description Some payees take a different category each time. The rule is still
Description worth having, because it is what makes the report readable.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given payee rules are : PayeeRules
| Pattern | MatchType | Payee  | Category |
| AMAZON  | Contains  | Amazon | none     |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount | FitId | Name                    | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2024-02-01 | -89.99 | A1    | AMAZON MKTPLACE PMTS WA | none | none     |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee  | Account                | Amount | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-02-01 | Amazon | Assets:Checking        | -89.99 | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-02-01 | Amazon | Expenses:Uncategorized | 89.99  | Uncleared |
# The side the statement is for is cleared, because the bank has reported it.
# The category has not been reported by anyone and is not.

Scenario With no rule the raw name is kept
Description Nothing is invented. The raw name is what the bank said, and it is
Description kept so that a rule written later can still match it.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given payee rules are : PayeeRules
| Pattern | MatchType | Payee | Category |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount | FitId | Name              | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2024-02-02 | -12.00 | A2    | SOME SHOP 4471 NC | none | none     |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee             | Account                | Amount | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-02-02 | SOME SHOP 4471 NC | Assets:Checking        | -12.00 | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-02-02 | SOME SHOP 4471 NC | Expenses:Uncategorized | 12.00 | Uncleared |

Scenario The raw name is kept even after a rule has renamed the payee
Description A rule written next year must be able to match what the bank sent
Description this year, so the original text is stored alongside the tidied name
Description rather than replaced by it.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given payee rules are : PayeeRules
| Pattern       | MatchType | Payee         | Category           |
| HARRIS TEETER | Contains  | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount | FitId       | Name                         | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2024-01-15 | -45.00 | 20240115001 | HARRIS TEETER #231 DURHAM NC | none | none     |
Then transaction names are : PayeeNames Vertical
| Payee   | Harris Teeter                |
| RawName | HARRIS TEETER #231 DURHAM NC |

Attributes PayeeNames
| Attribute | Type      | Default |
| Payee     | PayeeName | none    |
| RawName   | Text      | none    |

Scenario The most specific of two matching rules is applied
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Shopping      | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given payee rules are : PayeeRules
| Pattern         | MatchType | Payee           | Category           |
| AMAZON          | Contains  | Amazon          | Expenses:Shopping  |
| AMAZON GROCERY  | Contains  | Amazon Grocery  | Expenses:Groceries |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount | FitId | Name                    | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2024-02-03 | -31.00 | A3    | AMAZON GROCERY SUBS WA  | none | none     |
| DEBIT   | 2024-02-04 | -89.99 | A4    | AMAZON MKTPLACE PMTS WA | none | none     |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee          | Account            | Amount | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-02-03 | Amazon Grocery | Assets:Checking    | -31.00 | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-02-03 | Amazon Grocery | Expenses:Groceries | 31.00 | Uncleared |
| T2  | 2024-02-04 | Amazon         | Assets:Checking    | -89.99 | Cleared   |
| T2  | 2024-02-04 | Amazon         | Expenses:Shopping  | 89.99 | Uncleared |

Scenario A disabled rule is passed over
Description Rules are kept rather than deleted, because a rule that was wrong
Description once is usually wanted again in a changed form.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Shopping      | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given payee rules are : PayeeRules
| Pattern | MatchType | Payee  | Category          | Enabled |
| AMAZON  | Contains  | Amazon | Expenses:Shopping | false   |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount | FitId | Name                    | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2024-02-04 | -89.99 | A4    | AMAZON MKTPLACE PMTS WA | none | none     |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee                   | Account                | Amount | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-02-04 | AMAZON MKTPLACE PMTS WA | Assets:Checking        | -89.99 | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-02-04 | AMAZON MKTPLACE PMTS WA | Expenses:Uncategorized | 89.99 | Uncleared |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A rule that carries a split
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Entity PayeeSplitRule
Description A rule whose category side is several categories in fixed proportion.
Details A mortgage payment is the case this exists for: the same division of
Details interest, principal and escrow every month, with the amounts changing.
| Attribute | Type        | Default | Notes                              |
| Pattern   | Text        |         |                                    |
| MatchType | MatchType   | Contains |                                   |
| Payee     | PayeeName   |         |                                    |
| Shape     | SplitShape  | Amounts | How the lines divide the total     |

DataType SplitShape
Description Whether the remembered split lines are amounts or proportions.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value       | Notes                                                       |
| Amounts     | The amounts are reused as they are; any shortfall is queried |
| Percentages | The total is divided in the remembered proportions          |

Attributes RememberedSplitLine
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Category  | AccountPath |         |
| Amount    | Money       | 0.00    |
| Percent   | Decimal     | 0       |
| Memo      | Text        | none    |

Scenario A remembered split in fixed amounts is offered
Description The escrow and principal are the amounts last used. The total of the
Description downloaded transaction differs, so the shortfall is reported rather
Description than silently absorbed into one line.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                      | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking           | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Interest    | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Escrow      | Expense | false       |
| Liabilities:Mortgage      | Liability | false     |
| Expenses:Uncategorized    | Expense | false       |
Given payee split rules are : PayeeSplitRule Vertical
| Pattern   | FIRST NATIONAL MTG |
| MatchType | Contains           |
| Payee     | First National     |
| Shape     | Amounts            |
Given remembered split lines are : RememberedSplitLine
| Category               | Amount  | Memo      |
| Expenses:Home:Interest | 820.00  | Interest  |
| Liabilities:Mortgage   | 430.00  | Principal |
| Expenses:Home:Escrow   | 250.00  | Escrow    |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount   | FitId | Name                   | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2024-03-01 | -1500.00 | M1    | FIRST NATIONAL MTG ACH | none | none     |
Then split offered is : OfferedSplit
| Category               | Amount  | Memo      |
| Expenses:Home:Interest | 820.00  | Interest  |
| Liabilities:Mortgage   | 430.00  | Principal |
| Expenses:Home:Escrow   | 250.00  | Escrow    |
Then split shortfall is : SplitShortfall Vertical
| Total     | 1500.00 |
| Allocated | 1500.00 |
| Shortfall | 0.00    |
| Balanced  | true    |

Attributes OfferedSplit
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Category  | AccountPath |         |
| Amount    | Money       | 0.00    |
| Memo      | Text        | none    |

Attributes SplitShortfall
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Total     | Money   | 0.00    |
| Allocated | Money   | 0.00    |
| Shortfall | Money   | 0.00    |
| Balanced  | Boolean | false   |

Scenario A remembered split whose amounts no longer add up reports the shortfall
Description The payment went up by twenty dollars in January, as it does. The
Description remembered amounts are offered unchanged and the difference is
Description shown, for the user to put where it belongs.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                      | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking           | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Interest    | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Escrow      | Expense | false       |
| Liabilities:Mortgage      | Liability | false     |
| Expenses:Uncategorized    | Expense | false       |
Given payee split rules are : PayeeSplitRule Vertical
| Pattern   | FIRST NATIONAL MTG |
| MatchType | Contains           |
| Payee     | First National     |
| Shape     | Amounts            |
Given remembered split lines are : RememberedSplitLine
| Category               | Amount  | Memo      |
| Expenses:Home:Interest | 820.00  | Interest  |
| Liabilities:Mortgage   | 430.00  | Principal |
| Expenses:Home:Escrow   | 250.00  | Escrow    |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount   | FitId | Name                   | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2025-01-01 | -1520.00 | M2    | FIRST NATIONAL MTG ACH | none | none     |
Then split shortfall is : SplitShortfall Vertical
| Total     | 1520.00 |
| Allocated | 1500.00 |
| Shortfall | 20.00   |
| Balanced  | false   |

Scenario A remembered split in percentages divides the total
Description Percentages are applied to the downloaded total, so the lines always
Description add up. The rounding goes to the largest line, so that the sum is
Description exact and the adjustment lands where it is least visible.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                    | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking         | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Office:Rent    | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Office:Utility | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized  | Expense | false       |
Given payee split rules are : PayeeSplitRule Vertical
| Pattern   | OFFICE PARK LLC |
| MatchType | Contains        |
| Payee     | Office Park     |
| Shape     | Percentages     |
Given remembered split lines are : RememberedSplitLine
| Category                | Percent | Memo    |
| Expenses:Office:Rent    | 70      | Rent    |
| Expenses:Office:Utility | 30      | Utility |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount   | FitId | Name            | Memo | CheckNum |
| DEBIT   | 2024-03-01 | -1000.01 | O1    | OFFICE PARK LLC | none | none     |
Then split offered is : OfferedSplit
| Category                | Amount | Memo    |
| Expenses:Office:Rent    | 700.01 | Rent    |
| Expenses:Office:Utility | 300.00 | Utility |
Then split shortfall is : SplitShortfall Vertical
| Total     | 1000.01 |
| Allocated | 1000.01 |
| Shortfall | 0.00    |
| Balanced  | true    |

BusinessRule How a percentage split rounds
Description Each line is rounded to the cent, and whatever is left over after
Description that is added to the largest line.
Examples: PercentageSplit
| Total   | PercentA | PercentB | AmountA | AmountB | Notes                       |
| 1000.00 | 70       | 30       | 700.00  | 300.00  | Exact                       |
| 1000.01 | 70       | 30       | 700.01  | 300.00  | The odd cent to the larger  |
| 100.00  | 33       | 67       | 33.00   | 67.00   | Exact                       |
| 10.00   | 33       | 33       | 3.34    | 3.30    | Percentages need not total 100 |

Attributes PercentageSplit
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Total     | Money   | 0.00    |
| PercentA  | Decimal | 0       |
| PercentB  | Decimal | 0       |
| AmountA   | Money   | 0.00    |
| AmountB   | Money   | 0.00    |
| Notes     | String  | none    |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Learning a rule
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario Categorising an uncategorised transaction offers to remember it
Description This is how the rules get written: by doing the work once. The offer
Description is made, not taken, because the user is the only one who knows
Description whether this payee always takes this category.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee              | Account                | Amount |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | HARRIS TEETER #231 | Assets:Checking        | -45.00 |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | HARRIS TEETER #231 | Expenses:Uncategorized | 45.00  |
When category changed : CategoryChange Vertical
| Ref         | T1                     |
| FromAccount | Expenses:Uncategorized |
| ToAccount   | Expenses:Groceries     |
Then rule offered is : PayeeRule Vertical
| Pattern   | HARRIS TEETER #231 |
| MatchType | Contains           |
| Payee     | HARRIS TEETER #231 |
| Category  | Expenses:Groceries |
| Enabled   | true               |
# The pattern offered is the whole raw name, which is safe but narrow. The
# user shortens it to HARRIS TEETER, which is the point of offering it in an
# editable form rather than just writing it.

Scenario Accepting a learned rule categorises the transactions already there
Description Twenty years of imported history is mostly uncategorised. A rule is
Description only useful if it can be applied backwards, so accepting one offers
Description to do exactly that, and says how many it would change.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee                    | Account                | Amount |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | HARRIS TEETER #231       | Assets:Checking        | -45.00 |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | HARRIS TEETER #231       | Expenses:Uncategorized | 45.00  |
| T2  | 2024-02-20 | HARRIS TEETER #418 CARY  | Assets:Checking        | -62.10 |
| T2  | 2024-02-20 | HARRIS TEETER #418 CARY  | Expenses:Uncategorized | 62.10  |
| T3  | 2024-03-02 | SHELL OIL 4471           | Assets:Checking        | -52.00 |
| T3  | 2024-03-02 | SHELL OIL 4471           | Expenses:Uncategorized | 52.00  |
When rule applied to existing : PayeeRule Vertical
| Pattern   | HARRIS TEETER      |
| MatchType | Contains           |
| Payee     | Harris Teeter      |
| Category  | Expenses:Groceries |
Then would change : RuleApplication Vertical
| Matched        | 2 |
| AlreadyCorrect | 0 |
| Skipped        | 1 |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee          | Account                | Amount | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter  | Assets:Checking        | -45.00 | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter  | Expenses:Groceries     | 45.00 | Uncleared |
| T2  | 2024-02-20 | Harris Teeter  | Assets:Checking        | -62.10 | Cleared   |
| T2  | 2024-02-20 | Harris Teeter  | Expenses:Groceries     | 62.10 | Uncleared |
| T3  | 2024-03-02 | SHELL OIL 4471 | Assets:Checking        | -52.00 | Cleared   |
| T3  | 2024-03-02 | SHELL OIL 4471 | Expenses:Uncategorized | 52.00 | Uncleared |

Attributes RuleApplication
| Attribute      | Type    | Default |
| Matched        | Integer | 0       |
| AlreadyCorrect | Integer | 0       |
| Skipped        | Integer | 0       |

Scenario Applying a rule backwards does not touch a transaction already categorised
Description A category chosen by hand is a decision, and a rule applied in bulk
Description does not overrule it. Only Uncategorized postings are changed.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Dining        | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee              | Account                | Amount |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | HARRIS TEETER #231 | Assets:Checking        | -45.00 |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | HARRIS TEETER #231 | Expenses:Uncategorized | 45.00  |
| T2  | 2024-02-20 | HARRIS TEETER #418 | Assets:Checking        | -8.50  |
| T2  | 2024-02-20 | HARRIS TEETER #418 | Expenses:Dining        | 8.50   |
When rule applied to existing : PayeeRule Vertical
| Pattern   | HARRIS TEETER      |
| MatchType | Contains           |
| Payee     | Harris Teeter      |
| Category  | Expenses:Groceries |
Then would change : RuleApplication Vertical
| Matched        | 1 |
| AlreadyCorrect | 0 |
| Skipped        | 1 |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee         | Account            | Amount | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Assets:Checking    | -45.00 | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | 45.00 | Uncleared |
| T2  | 2024-02-20 | Harris Teeter | Assets:Checking    | -8.50 | Cleared   |
| T2  | 2024-02-20 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Dining    | 8.50 | Uncleared |
# The payee of T2 is tidied, because that is not a judgement. Its category is
# left, because it is.

Scenario A rule does not change a reconciled posting
Description A bulk change that altered a reconciled balance would undo a
Description reconciliation nobody asked it to touch.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries     | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee              | Account                | Amount | Cleared    |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | HARRIS TEETER #231 | Assets:Checking        | -45.00 | Reconciled |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | HARRIS TEETER #231 | Expenses:Uncategorized | 45.00  | Reconciled |
When rule applied to existing : PayeeRule Vertical
| Pattern   | HARRIS TEETER      |
| MatchType | Contains           |
| Payee     | Harris Teeter      |
| Category  | Expenses:Groceries |
Then would change : RuleApplication Vertical
| Matched        | 0 |
| AlreadyCorrect | 0 |
| Skipped        | 1 |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The payee list
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario The payee list is what the transactions use, not a separate table
Description There is no list of payees to maintain. The list offered for
Description autocompletion is the distinct payees of the transactions, most
Description recently used first, which means it needs no upkeep and can never
Description disagree with the register.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
Given postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee         | Account         | Amount |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Assets:Checking | -45.00 |
| T2  | 2024-02-20 | Shell         | Assets:Checking | -52.00 |
| T3  | 2024-03-02 | Harris Teeter | Assets:Checking | -31.00 |
Then payee list is : PayeeListEntry
| Payee         | LastUsed   | Count |
| Harris Teeter | 2024-03-02 | 2     |
| Shell         | 2024-02-20 | 1     |

Attributes PayeeListEntry
| Attribute | Type      | Default    |
| Payee     | PayeeName | none       |
| LastUsed  | Date      | 2024-01-01 |
| Count     | Integer   | 0          |

Scenario Choosing a payee offers the category it took last time
Description The last category used for a payee is the best guess there is, and
Description it costs nothing to offer because the register already knows it.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Dining    | Expense | false       |
Given postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee         | Account            | Amount |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Assets:Checking    | -45.00 |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | 45.00  |
| T2  | 2024-02-20 | Harris Teeter | Assets:Checking    | -8.50  |
| T2  | 2024-02-20 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Dining    | 8.50   |
When payee typed : PayeeSelect
| Payee         |
| Harris Teeter |
Then category suggested is : CategorySuggestion Vertical
| Category | Expenses:Dining |
| BasedOn  | 2024-02-20      |

Attributes CategorySuggestion
| Attribute | Type        | Default    |
| Category  | AccountPath | none       |
| BasedOn   | Date        | 2024-01-01 |

Scenario A payee renamed everywhere keeps its history together
Description Twenty years of imports leave the same shop under several spellings.
Description Merging them is a rename applied to every transaction, and it is
Description what makes a report by payee worth reading.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
Given postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee              | Account         | Amount |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | HARRIS TEETER #231 | Assets:Checking | -45.00 |
| T2  | 2024-02-20 | Harris Teeter      | Assets:Checking | -62.10 |
| T3  | 2024-03-02 | harris teeter 418  | Assets:Checking | -31.00 |
When payees merged : PayeeMerge Vertical
| NewName | Harris Teeter |
Then payee list is : PayeeListEntry
| Payee         | LastUsed   | Count |
| Harris Teeter | 2024-03-02 | 3     |

Attributes PayeeMerge
| Attribute | Type      | Default |
| NewName   | PayeeName |         |
